  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now focusing on Step 4.2 and Step 5.1.1 of the `cloudsql-setup` process, considering our environment\'s constraints. My approach involves installing dependencies judiciously using a batched `install_applet_package` call. This call will include all required runtime and `@types/*` packages within a single `PackageNames` array, setting `IsDevDependency` to false.\n\n\n**Consolidating Package Installs**\n\nI\'ve determined the necessary packages: `firebase`, `firebase-admin`, `drizzle-orm`, `drizzle-kit`, `pg`, and `@types/pg`. I\'ll use a single `install_applet_package` call to install them as runtime dependencies. The task is to write several files, including `src/db/schema.ts` and `src/lib/firebase.ts`, in parallel with the installation process, given that compilation and server restart are deferred until installation completes.\n\n\n**Designing Schema First**\n\nI\'m now carefully designing `src/db/schema.ts` to integrate seamlessly with both the `cloudsql-setup` requirements and the specific needs of **Pit Box Fantasy**. The focus is on the `users` table, ensuring the Firebase Auth `uid` is the unique identifier, as stated in the documentation. I\'ve considered all of the requested attributes, including `id`, `uid`, `email`, `name`, `avatar`, `last_year_rank`, `role`, and `cumulative_prior_points`. I will ensure that the datatypes and constraints, such as `notNull()` and `default()`, are all appropriate.\n\n\n**Defining User and Series**\n\nI\'m now integrating Firebase Auth `uid` to the user schema. My goal is to ensure a unique key, and I\'ll include `.unique()` on the `users.uid` column to enable clean `onConflictDoUpdate` operations. I\'m also now designing the `series_config` table; I have started with the columns: `id`, `name`, `badgeColor`, `maxWeeklyUsesPerDriver`, `chaseActive`, and `firstPlacePoints`.\n\n\n**Constructing Driver & Race Tables**\n\nI\'m expanding the schema, now focusing on the `drivers` and `races` tables. I\'ve defined columns for both, including `id`, `nascarDriverId`, `seriesId`, `carNumber`, and `fullName`. For the `drivers` table, I\'m including `team`, several boolean flags like `madeLastYearChase` and `inCurrentChase`, and `active`. For `races`, I\'ve specified `nascarRaceId`, and established relationships via `seriesId`.\n\n\n**Adding Race & Roster Tables**\n\nI\'m expanding the schema further, now adding the `races` and `season_rosters` tables, as well as the `weekly_picks` table. For races, I\'ve specified columns like `weekendGroupId`, `raceName`, `trackName`, `raceStartTime`, `isChaseRace`, `syncStatus`, and `lastSyncedAt`. For season rosters, I\'ve defined `userId`, `seriesId`, `slotType`, `driverId`, and `fallbackCarNumber`, with appropriate foreign key relationships. The weekly picks are next.\n\n\n\n**Defining Weekly Picks**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the `weekly_picks` table schema, carefully considering its relationships. I\'ve defined `userId`, `raceId`, `seriesId`, `driverId`, and `isHistorical` as foreign keys to their respective tables, ensuring a clear and efficient data structure. I\'m also including `trackLabel` and `submittedAt`, and I\'ve established `primaryKey()` on the composite key. I\'m also working on the `race_driver_results` table.\n\n\n"
