  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Database Errors**\n\nI\'m currently focused on dissecting the database errors flagged in the metadata, specifically the failed insertion into \"race_driver_results\" during the `syncRaceResultsToDatabase` operation. My primary question is why this insertion failed. I am investigating the specific DrizzleQueryError and tracing its origin.\n\n\n**Investigating Insertion Failure**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the `syncRaceResultsToDatabase` operation and the unique index `idx_race_nascar_driver` on `race_driver_results`. The key seems to be concurrent requests. It seems like multiple requests could trigger the `syncRaceResultsToDatabase` function simultaneously. This leads to both deleting and inserting the same `(race_id, nascar_driver_id)` rows concurrently, causing unique constraint violations.\n\n\n**Refining Conflict Resolution**\n\nI\'m now thinking the absence of `.onConflictDoUpdate(...)` is the root cause of the unique constraint violation (`23505`). I\'m also considering the implications if `DELETE` is restricted for `SQL_USER`. The safer route seems to be using `.onConflictDoUpdate({ target: [raceDriverResults.raceId, raceDriverResults.nascarDriverId], set: { ... } })` on the insert operation, especially given the concurrent request issue. I also need to check permissions on `SQL_USER` in case this caused the problem. Finally, I will check the `simulateZilischInjury` toggle.\n\n\n**Addressing Roster Conflicts**\n\nI\'ve shifted focus to `error 5` and `error 6`, specifically the \"Exclusive Draft Rule\" violations in `updateSeasonRosterSlot` and `updateSeasonRoster`. I\'ve realized the problem lies in Tab 4, the Season Roster UI, within `src/App.tsx`. The dropdowns for exclusive slots don\'t indicate or swap drafted drivers, leading to conflicts. This means the dropdown lists *all* eligible drivers, regardless of prior selections by other managers, causing the errors.\n\n\n**Improving Driver Selection**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the \"Exclusive Draft Rule\" violations specifically within the context of the prototype league\'s pre-seeded drivers and managers. I\'ve realized the core issue is the restricted pool of available drivers in the season roster dropdowns. If a manager tries to change a driver, every other driver in the dropdown is already owned, due to how the league was pre-populated, triggering database errors. The proposed solutions are to add more undrafted drivers across series and ensure the dropdowns reflect draft status accurately.\n\n\n**Implementing Driver Lockout**\n\nI\'m now implementing driver lockout for exclusive slots (1-4). When a user picks a driver already selected by another manager, that driver will be disabled in the `<select>` dropdown, with the label \"Locked by [Manager Name]\". Adding several undrafted drivers across series is also key to avoiding the exclusive draft rule violations.\n\n\n**Enhancing Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on streamlining error handling. I\'m going to ensure that draft validation rules don\'t log `console.error` if a user attempts to draft a locked driver via the `/api/season-roster` endpoint, or if they swap during commissioner mode. Furthermore, I\'ll review `server.ts` and `src/db/repository.ts` for other potential `console.error` triggers. I am specifically looking at the `syncRaceResultsToDatabase` function to use `.onConflictDoUpdate` to prevent race conditions during database updates.\n\n\n**Seeding Open Drivers**\n\nI\'m now implementing the seeding of additional undrafted drivers. I plan to use `ExecuteSql` in PostgreSQL to ensure multiple open drivers are available for each series. This is key, especially given the draft board. I also intend to address the \"Locked by\" display in the Exclusive Draft selector for drivers already taken. This will make concurrent requests and simulations fully idempotent.\n\n\n"
